This is a strong mechanism draft, but I’d put it slightly below the weighted-pendulum Hugo version because this one’s mechanism is more complicated: wire, hinge, concealed lever, delayed chime, frozen hands. It works, but it needs very clean explanation to avoid feeling contrived.

The roles are clear:

Investigator: Eleanor Voss
Victim: Dr. Mallory Finch
Culprit: Beatrice Quill
False suspects: Captain Ivor Hale, Sylvia Trent, Hugo Vane
Murder method: stabbing with a letter opener
Cover-up mechanism: service-corridor door hinge pulls a hidden wire/lever that halts the grandfather clock and delays the chime
Key clues: clock frozen at 7:15, six-foot wire, concealed lever, hinge scratch, delayed chime, Beatrice’s clockmaker apprenticeship, maintenance notes in Beatrice’s hand, Beatrice’s staff-corridor access, fingerprints on lever
Motive: Beatrice killed to protect Captain Hale’s reputation

I’d score it around 84/100. The opening plants the body, stopped clock, dust, wire, scratch, and bloodied candlestick/weapon setup effectively, giving the story a strong physical mystery from the start.

